<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pennsylvania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pennsylvania is a state in the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It was founded in 1681 when Charles II granted the territory to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a>, who made it a refuge for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> and other religious groups. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, its main city, was where the Declaration of Independence and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a> were adopted, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gettysburg">Battle of Gettysburg</a> was fought in the state in 1863.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pennsylvania.jpg" alt="Pennsylvania" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simtropolitan, Jean-Pierre Demailly (old version) permission= Insignia This image shows a flag, a coat of arms, a sea... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Pennsylvania.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pennsylvania is officially the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania. It lies in the Mid-Atlantic, Northeastern, Appalachian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> regions, and is bordered by Delaware, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>, West Virginia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> and, across Lake Erie, the Canadian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>. The Mason-Dixon line forms its southern boundary. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachian Mountains</a> run through its centre, and about 59 per cent of the state is forested. Of the original Thirteen Colonies, it is the only one that does not border the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>, though it has waterfront on Lake Erie and the tidal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Humans lived in what is now Pennsylvania for thousands of years before the colony was founded. The Meadowcroft Rockshelter in Jefferson Township holds the earliest known signs of human activity in the state and perhaps in all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, and may pre-date the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> culture. By 1000 CE the native population had developed agricultural techniques and a mixed food economy. Between 1638 and 1655 a south-eastern portion of the state was part of New Sweden, a Swedish colony.</p><p>In 1681 a royal land grant gave the territory to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a>, son of the man after whom it is named. The colonial Province of Pennsylvania was known for relatively peaceful relations with native peoples, an innovative system of government and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-pluralism">religious pluralism</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> hosted the First and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-continental-congress">Second Continental Congress</a>; the latter formed the Continental Army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> in 1775 and unanimously adopted the Declaration of Independence the following year. On 12 December 1787 Pennsylvania was the second state to ratify the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a>, after Delaware. It has been known as the Keystone State since 1802, for its central place among the original colonies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital is Harrisburg, and the largest cities are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and Pittsburgh. The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 13,011,844 residents. The state has had five constitutions, in 1776, 1790, 1838, 1874 and 1968. Its industrial past gave it the lesser nicknames of the Coal State, the Oil State and the Steel State, and its five manufacturing centres were Philadelphia, Pittsburgh, Erie, Scranton-Wilkes-Barre and the Lehigh Valley.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pennsylvania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Repeated wars in the seventeenth century devastated the land, and in the eighteenth century poverty and religious pressure drove thousands of Palatines to emigrate, many to <b>Pennsylvania</b> and New York, where they formed a large part of the Pennsylvania Dutch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1870 Charles Taze Russell and others formed a group in Pittsburgh, <b>Pennsylvania</b>, to study the Bible, and Russell came to dispute several tenets of mainstream Christianity, among them the immortality of the soul, hellfire, predestination and the Trinity.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jehovahs-witnesses">Jehovah's Witnesses · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1688 residents of Germantown, <b>Pennsylvania</b>, issued the first organised protest against slavery in the English colonies, and in the 18th century John Woolman and Anthony Benezet persuaded the Society of Friends to expel members who traded in slaves.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>West Jersey and <b>Pennsylvania</b> were established by William Penn in 1676 and 1682, and he signed a peace treaty with Tamanend, leader of the Lenape; the peace endured almost a century, until the Penn's Creek Massacre of 1755.</blockquote><a href="/movement/quakers">Quakers · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>James Wilson of <b>Pennsylvania</b> argued for a single executive chosen by the people, holding that one person would be more energetic and more clearly accountable than a council, while Edmund Randolph warned that a single executive would be “the foetus of monarchy”.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Quaker William Penn, founder of the Province of <b>Pennsylvania</b>, employed an anti-militarist public policy, and the colony was essentially unarmed for the 75 years from 1681 to 1756 and experienced little or no warfare.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pacifism">Pacifism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
